  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe new approach employs a randomized derivative-free method inspired by alloy discovery and optimization literature (Salnikov et al). We'll use Bayesian optimization with a multi-fidelity warping method to reduce the optimization dimension. The core idea is to represent the function h as a discretized wavelet basis and use projected gradient optimization with adaptive trust regions. This differs from the previous heuristic-based method by focusing on mathematical gradient derivation and dimensionality reduction through wavelet decomposition. The main bottleneck addressed is the high-dimensional optimization of 400 variables, which is reduced to 20-30 effective gradient dimensions. We also incorporate time-adaptive warm-up and cool-down phases to balance exploration vs exploitation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]\u207f with constraint \u2211h_i dx = 1 (dx=2/n)\n- Objective: minimize C\u2085 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx (computed via correlation)\n- Constraints: 0\u2264h_i\u22641, \u2211h_i = n/2\n- Solvers: Coordinate descent (FISTA-like) with adaptive step sizes, projected onto feasible set after each iteration using:\n  h_new = clip(h_old + \u03b4 + b\u22c5randn, 0,1) where b is regularization\n  then adjust sum by scaling\n- Gradient estimation: Use finite-differences but employs dimension-reduction via low-rank SVD approximation of the Hessian\n- Time adaptation: Dynamic projection scaling p(t) = 1/(1+exp(-t/T)) where T adapts based on improvement rate\n- Multi-fidelity: Use 1-dimensional Fourier basis for low-frequency components and sparse grid for high frequencies\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nStage 1: Dimension reduction & initialization\n- Project initial solution onto a low-dimensional (d=20) feature space using SPCA\n- Compute importance weights: w_i = |\u2202C\u2085/\u2202h_i| / \u2211|\u2202C\u2085/\u2202h_i|\n- Sort variables by weight, select top k=20\n- Compute gradient approximations using:\n  g_i \u2248 (C\u2085(h+\u03b4e_i) - C\u2085(h)) / \u03b4 for i in selected indices\n  (using adaptive \u03b4 = [1e-5, dx/5, 2*dx] based on scale)\n\nStage 2: Bayesian optimization with warm-up/cool-down\n- Use GP regression with Automatic Relevance Determination (ARD) kernels for each feature\n- Warm-up: Bayesian optimization with small trust regions until convergence plateau\n- Cool-down: Progressive dimension reduction by freezing least relevant variables (using relevance =  |sum_{k} \u2202C\u2085/\u2202h_i at lag k|\n- Optimization steps:\n  maximize -C\u2085(f) subject to sum(f)=0.5*n, 0\u2264f\u22641\n  use coin-or.nl_opt suite with barrier method\n  restart every 100 steps with perturbation\n\nStage 3: Global repair & multi-fidelity checkpointing:\n   if C\u2085 improvement rate < 1e-8:\n        fall back to multi-fidelity solution (10-point grid)\n        but this branches off into separate GP-BO run on reduced space\n\n4. Search choices:\n- Primary: Simultaneous coordinate stretching with projection (best for gradient-check avoidance)\n- Secondary: Fourier-initialized annealing (robust fallback)\n- Schedule: Warm-up: 200 steps, optimization: 800 steps, cool-down: 200 steps\n- Hyperparameter adaptation: Decreasing Cauchy noise based on improvement rate\n- Dimension selection: Adaptive dimension from 30 to 10 in steps of -2 when improvement stalls\n- Dimension evaluation metric: sum(|\u2202C\u2085/\u2202h_i|) stability < 1e-5\n\n5. Compute budget:\n- Warm-up: ~5% of wall time\n- Main optimization: 30% wall-time allocated to domain growth\n- Repair phases: 15% total wall time\n- Multi-fidelity checkpoint: 20% total time (when needed)\n- Cooldown: 10% wall time\n- Monitoring: Every 20 steps, check if max(\u2202C\u2085/\u2202h_i) gradient norm < preprocessing threshold\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n- Final solution sanitation:\n  - 0 \u2264 min(h) \u2264 max(h) \u2264 1\n  - |sum(h) dx - 1| < 1e-10\n  - C5 finite (computed via both corr and gradient-based)\n- Clean evaluation:\n  - Recompute C5 independently using np.correlate with mode=\"full\"\n  - Compare with smooth_surrogate version\n- Fall-back mechanism:\n  - Maintain best solution found during warm-up\n  - If optimization stalls >300 steps, return to best solution\n  - Use multi-fidelity grid only when warm-up shows limited progress\n\nExecution flow diagram (conceptual):\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500(1) Dimension reduction with SPCA\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502                                             \u2502\n\u2502 Decision tree to determine strategy choice:\n\u2502 \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2574\u2550\u2550\u25ba Random sampling grid\n\u2502 \u2502                             \u2502\n\u2502           1          2         3\n\u2502           \u25bc           \u25bc          \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500(3) Algorithm selection [Grid, Fourier, Wavelets]\u2510\n\u2502                                                    \u2502\n\u2502 Student-t process surrogate calibration           \u2502\n\u2502 Common tangential hyperplane descent             \u2502\n\u2502                                                    \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518",
